     1	# Original worlds: naming, art and launch rules
     2	
     3	Control Rod's Floor worlds are meant to be shared widely as open source. They must be fun to recognize as a *kind* of place and impossible to mistake for anyone else's property. This page is the rule set; `tools/check_originality.py` enforces the parts a machine can check.
     4	
     5	This is an engineering practice, not legal advice. If a rights holder ever writes, follow the response steps at the end and get a lawyer's view before arguing.
     6	
     7	## The line we stay on
     8	
     9	Ideas, genres and moods are free to use: a candy factory, a heist crew, a woodland guild, a space shipyard, a cozy café. What belongs to others is their **expression and their marks**: character names and designs, logos and emblems, catchphrases, lyrics, distinctive costumes, set layouts, and anything that makes a reader think a world is endorsed by or connected to them.
    10	
    11	## Naming rules (adjacent names)
    12	
    13	Use plain descriptive words, then make them vivid with our own invention.
    14	
    15	| Do | Don't |
    16	|---|---|
    17	| Generic place and genre words: Chocolate Factory, Heist Night, Starport, Lantern Woods | Any franchise, studio, character, place or product name |
    18	| Our own invented proper nouns: Tumbletown, Cloudspring, Lanternwood | Sound-alike or pun names that point at a mark (rhymes, one letter off, spoonerisms) |
    19	| One distinctive invented detail per world | A cluster of borrowed clues that together decode to one franchise |
    20	| Taglines about the work and the mood | Quoted or near-quoted catchphrases, lyrics or titles |
    21	| Comparisons by genre in docs ("a heist-movie mood") | "Like X", "inspired by X", "X-style" for any named franchise, anywhere, including READMEs, release notes, posts and video captions |
    22	
    23	Stable world IDs never change; display names and taglines may be refined.
    24	
    25	## Display names
    26	
    27	Adopted 2026-10-30 at the owner's direction. Earlier working titles were already generic; these push the fun further while staying on the line. IDs are unchanged.
    28	
    29	| ID | Former working title | Display name | Tagline |
    30	|---|---|---|---|
    31	| `confectionery-works` | Confectionery Works | **The Chocolate Factory** | Where every package gets a candy-coated inspection. |
    32	| `midnight-bureau` | Midnight Bureau | **Heist Night** | A crew of specialists, one careful plan, zero surprises. |
    33	| `creature-dispatch` | Creature Dispatch | **The Monster Mailroom** | Big, gentle creatures who never lose a parcel. |
    34	| `pocket-lab` | Pocket Lab | **Little Helpers Laboratory** | Tiny inventors, enormous equipment. |
    35	| `lanternwood-guild` | Lanternwood Guild | **The Lantern Woods Guild** | Mapmakers, smiths and couriers on a quest for done. |
    36	| `corner-cafe` | Corner Café | **The Coffee House Crew** | Good coffee, good company, everything shipped by Friday. |
    37	| `tumbletown-workshop` | Tumbletown Workshop | **Tumbletown Rhyme Works** | Impossible machines that somehow pass inspection. |
    38	| `orbital-shipyard` | Orbital Shipyard | **Starport Shipyard** | Robots, aliens and engineers keeping the fleet flying. |
    39	| `wildlight-camp` | Wildlight Research Camp | **Critter Expedition Camp** | Your field team is small, furry and very thorough. |
    40	| `voxel-foundry` | Voxel Foundry | **Blockworks Foundry** | Every deliverable built one cube at a time. |
    41	| `cloudspring-bathhouse` | Cloudspring Bathhouse | **The Cloudspring Baths** | Porcelain attendants, warm steam and very careful records. |
    42	| `button-and-spool` | Button & Spool | **Thimble Town** | A tiny workshop hidden inside the sewing drawer. |
    43	| `clockwork-observatory` | Clockwork Observatory | **Clockwork Stargazers** | Brass automatons charting your work across the sky. |
    44	| `coral-commons` | Coral Commons | **Reef Town** | An octopus, a seahorse and a very organized crab. |
    45	| `meadow-post` | Meadow Post | **Cozy Critter Village** | Badgers, mice and a hedgehog who checks everything twice. |
    46	
    47	Notes on the edges: "The Chocolate Factory" stays safe only if nothing else in the world adds that story's specific clues (no golden tickets, no boat on a chocolate river, no small workers in matching outfits). The café world avoids a fixed group size and signature furniture for the same reason. "Suffix -craft" is avoided for the voxel world because it points at one game.
    48	
    49	## Art rules
    50	
    51	Already in `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-25-overlay-themes-catalog.md` and enforced in review:
    52	
    53	- Original faces, proportions, silhouettes and costumes; no likeness of any existing character or performer.
    54	- No emblems, insignia, logos, game interfaces, signature props or set layouts from existing works.
    55	- No combination of colour, silhouette and costume that recalls an existing mascot.
    56	- Artwork is generated in this repository (`tools/build_theme_art.py`) and released under Apache-2.0 with a per pack `NOTICE.txt`. No reference images are committed.
    57	- Reviewer step before a pack is released: view the cast at full size beside the genre's best known works and ask "would a stranger name a specific franchise?" If yes, change the design.
    58	
    59	## Launch and marketing
    60	
    61	Viral posts are where projects get into trouble. In the README, release notes, demos, screenshots, videos and social posts:
    62	
    63	- Describe worlds by our names and genres only. Never "[franchise]-style", "our [franchise] world" or similar, even jokingly.
    64	- Do not use franchise hashtags, music, sound effects, fonts or logos.
    65	- Do not invite comparisons ("guess which movie this is").
    66	
    67	## Repository history
    68	
    69	Earlier commits on the `feat/overlay-themes` branch contain franchise names in internal notes. Merge this pull request with **squash** so the target branch carries only the cleaned text. GitHub keeps pull request history, so the names remain visible in this PR's commits; rewriting that history is an owner decision.
    70	
    71	## If a rights holder writes
    72	
    73	1. Acknowledge promptly and politely.
    74	2. Disable the named world in the next release (worlds are data; removal is one entry).
    75	3. Record the request and the action in the change record.
    76	4. Get legal advice before restoring or contesting.
     1	# Fifteen-world catalog and art-production specification
     2	
     3	Date: 2026-10-25. Status: proposed art briefs; every title is a working title and every pack starts **development**. No artwork, trademark clearance or license is supplied by this document.
     4	
     5	Read with the [engine specification](2026-10-25-overlay-themes-design.md) and [implementation plan](../plans/2026-10-25-overlay-themes.md). Franchise references below are explicit **creative lineage**, not approved public branding, permissions or an instruction to copy protected expression. Public influence notes are optional and separately reviewed. Recognition through atmosphere is a goal; exact source recognition is not an acceptance test that overrides distinct original expression.
     6	
     7	## Shared art recipe
     8	
     9	Every pack supplies one scene, six ordered character variants, up to six reusable prop assets, a thumbnail, descriptor, editable source and asset/rights manifest. See engine section 6 for dimensions, byte budgets, passive-SVG rules and common action treatments. Six variants do not cap the number of agents. Stable run badges distinguish repeated variants.
    10	
    11	Every scene places these semantic stations in the existing floor's corresponding zones: **general** (upper left), **site** (upper middle), **archive** (upper right), **verify** (lower left), **office** (lower middle), **bench** (lower right). Pack-specific station names below are decorative nicknames, not replacements for canonical labels. Keep task labels on quiet backplates. No scenery may conceal a slot, focus ring or request marker.
    12	
    13	Characters share a foot anchor but not a body template: mix silhouettes, material, proportions and tools. Each supports shared work/inspect/wait/attention/submitted/error treatments. Eyes, gestures and held tools should read at scene size. No visual disability, species, costume or character variant encodes actual model quality, permission, competence or task success.
    14	
    15	Each signature below uses one existing shared primitive; a unique illustration and palette provide the difference. Effects occur only on observed submission and never indicate that human approval happened. Accepted packages get the shared static archive seal. No random deliveries masquerade as real outputs.
    16	
    17	### Production waves and coverage
    18	
    19	| Wave | IDs | Distinct experience tested |
    20	|---|---|---|
    21	| A | `creature-dispatch`, `pocket-lab` | Creature silhouettes, mechanical workers, busy but readable scenes |
    22	| B | `midnight-bureau` | Human silhouettes, restrained motion, premium dark scene |
    23	| C | `confectionery-works`, `lanternwood-guild`, `corner-cafe`, `tumbletown-workshop` | Fantasy, domestic warmth and hand-drawn whimsy |
    24	| D | `orbital-shipyard`, `wildlight-camp`, `voxel-foundry` | Sci-fi, creature variety and geometric construction |
    25	| E | `cloudspring-bathhouse`, `button-and-spool`, `clockwork-observatory`, `coral-commons`, `meadow-post` | Calm, miniatures, celestial craft, underwater life and pastel comfort |
    26	
    27	## 1. The Monster Mailroom
    28	
    29	- **Genre:** friendly monster workplace ensembles; a busy dispatch floor run by gentle creatures. Wave A. ID `creature-dispatch`.
    30	- **Emotional target:** a bustling, warmhearted creature workplace; capable workers with comic physical variety.
    31	- **Composition/materials:** stepped parcel depot, frosted capsule tubes, ceramic counters, ribbed metal, soft overhead pools of light. Emphasize parcel routing; avoid any apparatus or set piece recognizable from an existing work.
    32	- **Art swatches:** seafoam `#78C5AF`, plum `#60476E`, butter `#F4D79A`, ink `#20323D`. Functional text contrast is checked separately.
    33	- **Cast order:** broad moss-furred courier; tall velvet moth clerk; squat stone sorter; long-necked feather archivist; round many-footed mechanic; small fin-eared inspector. Original faces and proportions; no likeness of any existing character, costume or insignia.
    34	- **Stations:** dispatch desk / fabrication dock / parcel archive / inspection counter / supervisor window / waiting platform (order: general/site/archive/verify/office/bench).
    35	- **Props:** parcel capsule, routing card, desk stamp, trolley, magnifier, tool pouch.
    36	- **Submission signature:** `capsule`, illustrated as a parcel entering a local receiving tray. Waiting workers hold a routing card; failed/lost states become still, attentive poses with the shared clear label.
    37	- **Candidate public description:** “An extraordinary crew keeps your work moving through a lively dispatch depot.”
    38	
    39	## 2. Little Helpers Laboratory
    40	
    41	- **Genre:** small-helper ensemble comedy; retro scientific workshops. Wave A. ID `pocket-lab`.
    42	- **Emotional target:** mischievous invention with readable, purposeful activity.
    43	- **Composition/materials:** cutaway laboratory, curved enamel worktops, chunky switches, instrument racks, borosilicate vessels. Amber task lights provide warmth without making workers a yellow-uniform swarm.
    44	- **Art swatches:** cobalt `#355C9B`, warm white `#EEE8DA`, coral `#E37E6A`, graphite `#253440`.
    45	- **Cast order:** cube-bodied display assistant; three-legged copper instrument; accordion-neck robot; ceramic sphere on tracks; articulated clamp helper; tiny multi-drawer automaton. Avoid any silhouette, colour and costume combination that recalls an existing mascot, and any copied voice.
    46	- **Stations:** experiment desk / prototype bench / specimen cabinet / calibration bay / lead scientist desk / charging nook.
    47	- **Props:** blueprint, coil, caliper, sample tray, work lamp, small trolley.
    48	- **Submission signature:** `spark`, a brief contained instrument glow rather than an explosion or evidence-of-success claim.
    49	- **Candidate description:** “Small inventors, oversized equipment, and a laboratory full of personality.”
    50	
    51	## 3. Heist Night
    52	
    53	- **Genre:** ensemble heist cinema; a calm, competent crew planning at night. Wave B. ID `midnight-bureau`.
    54	- **Emotional target:** calm competence, specialization and sophisticated teamwork.
    55	- **Composition/materials:** city-night planning office, walnut, brass, frosted glass, blue-lit drafting tables. Functional layers stay brighter than the moody background.
    56	- **Art swatches:** midnight `#172A3A`, champagne `#CDB78F`, walnut `#735743`, paper `#F0E8DB`.
    57	- **Cast order:** planner with rolled drawings; communications specialist with headset; researcher with tabbed notebook; logistics specialist with case; analyst with tablet; inspector with loupe. Original faces/clothes; no actor likenesses, film dialogue, real venue logos or copied promotional layouts.
    58	- **Stations:** briefing table / operations desk / dossier vault / audit table / director desk / lounge.
    59	- **Props:** dossier, blueprint roll, task lamp, case, route card, tabletop projector.
    60	- **Submission signature:** `dossier`, a crisp folder placement. Working motions are small hand/tool movements rather than physical comedy.
    61	- **Candidate description:** “A specialist crew turns a careful plan into coordinated work.”
    62	
