     7	## Shared art recipe
     8	
     9	Every pack supplies one scene, six ordered character variants, up to six reusable prop assets, a thumbnail, descriptor, editable source and asset/rights manifest. See engine section 6 for dimensions, byte budgets, passive-SVG rules and common action treatments. Six variants do not cap the number of agents. Stable run badges distinguish repeated variants.
    10	
    11	Every scene places these semantic stations in the existing floor's corresponding zones: **general** (upper left), **site** (upper middle), **archive** (upper right), **verify** (lower left), **office** (lower middle), **bench** (lower right). Pack-specific station names below are decorative nicknames, not replacements for canonical labels. Keep task labels on quiet backplates. No scenery may conceal a slot, focus ring or request marker.
    12	
    13	Characters share a foot anchor but not a body template: mix silhouettes, material, proportions and tools. Each supports shared work/inspect/wait/attention/submitted/error treatments. Eyes, gestures and held tools should read at scene size. No visual disability, species, costume or character variant encodes actual model quality, permission, competence or task success.
    14	
    15	Each signature below uses one existing shared primitive; a unique illustration and palette provide the difference. Effects occur only on observed submission and never indicate that human approval happened. Accepted packages get the shared static archive seal. No random deliveries masquerade as real outputs.
    16	
   207	## Art handoff, review and acceptance
   208	
   209	1. Artist starts with a six-silhouette sheet, environment composition, swatches and explicit influence/difference notes. Internal reference boards can name sources; retain references privately as appropriate, not copied media in shipped assets.
   210	2. Review scene with real actor hit boxes, labels and office markers overlaid before final detailing. Check six stations are independently readable in Full/Quiet/Still and grayscale.
   211	3. Produce editable originals and export passive SVG through the shared build gate. Supply manifest entries with creator, date, tool, input references, license/ownership evidence and output hashes. Copied references are not production assets.
   212	4. Run every pack through the same semantic contact-sheet fixture, keyboard/list browser checks, contrast check and size/motion budgets. Review at scene size; a beautiful enlarged character sheet alone is insufficient.
   213	5. Record original naming/art assessment, any licensed material, intended distribution and approved optional influence text. Resolve actual concerns for that pack; don't require new blanket permission merely for generic genre influence. Engineering validates record completeness, not legal sufficiency.
   214	6. Release only the reviewed asset hash/version bundle. A complete launch means fifteen eligible packs plus Plain Floor. A phased pilot can ship fewer, with remaining concepts accurately marked unavailable.
   215	
   216	All names, palettes, cast details and public descriptions are proposed design choices. The intent is vivid recognizable influence plus an original product identity, not one-for-one character substitutes disguised by new names.
